course: dictionary
lesson: 14144
title: Old words with a new key
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are rare today. You will meet them in old stories, but you will hardly hear them in the playground. «Misdo» is one of these words. Its parts are mis- (wrongly) and do (to act or make). Put them together and you get: to do something wrongly.
explain: «The apprentice misdid the spell, and the potion turned bright blue.» The apprentice did the spell wrongly, so the result went wrong. The clue is in the second half of the sentence: the potion went wrong because the spell was done badly.
explain: Today we say do something wrong or make a mistake instead. The noun «misdoings» is more common in old books. It means wrong actions, as in «His misdoings were soon found out by the whole village.»
fact: «Misdo» means to do something wrongly or badly.
fact: «Misdo» can also mean to do wrong, or to behave badly.
fact: Its forms are misdoes, misdid, misdone and misdoing, but you will rarely see them today.
fact: «Misdoings» means wrong actions, and it is more common than the verb.
fact: The prefix mis- means wrongly or badly, as in misread, mislead and misspell.
exercise: «The apprentice misdid the spell, and the potion turned bright blue.» What does misdid mean here? | Read the spell aloud | Finished the spell early | *Did the spell wrongly | Did the spell very slowly
exercise: Which everyday phrase means the same as misdo? | *Make a mistake | Go to bed | Give a gift | Shout loudly
exercise: «His misdoings were soon found out by the whole village.» What are misdoings? | Long journeys | *Wrong actions | Funny songs | Kind gifts
test: «The guard misdid his duty and let the thief pass.» What does misdid mean? | *Did his duty wrongly | Did his duty early | Polished his boots | Did his duty with great pride
test: Yesterday, the baker ___ the sum wrongly. Which word fits the gap? | misdone | misdoing | misdoes | *misdid
exam: Which sentence uses misdo with its meaning? | The clerk smiled at the window. | *The clerk misdid the sums, so the bill was wrong. | The clerk ran to the shop for milk. | The clerk sang a song at lunch.
